<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 25%;"><b>Scenario:</b> Intelligent Robots</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Theme:</b> How Technology Changes Society</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 25%;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 75%;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="3" style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Resolve misunderstandings in group projects. Communication: Aid in negotiating group roles.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can resolve misunderstandings about AI's impact on society by using simple terms and gestures to explain (e.g., uses hand motions to simulate learning and says, "AI is a technology that is programmed to learn from data and perform tasks like humans.")
            Can help assign roles in group projects on AI, using visuals or quick sketches to illustrate roles based on classmates' interests (e.g., "Would you like to focus on AI in education or in the workplace?" with icons representing each sector).
            Can clarify the group's goals for projects on AI's societal impacts, ensuring each role is understood with visual aids (e.g., "Our project will discuss how AI could change jobs and daily life," using a timeline of technology evolution for context).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students watch a 1-minute video clip showing a common group project scenario where there's a minor misunderstanding about a task or a role. After the video, the teacher asks, "What was the problem in the group? How could they have resolved it?" This activates prior knowledge on collaboration and sets the stage for mediation. Students share initial thoughts using terms like `challenges` or `misunderstandings`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models phrases for clarifying information and assigning roles. For example, "Let me clarify what Sarah meant by 'data collection' for our AI project," or "Perhaps we can assign roles based on interests. Who would like to focus on the `technological` `functionality` of `robots`, and who on their `societal` `impact`?" The teacher explicitly pronounces words like `/ˈɪn.ə.veɪ.tɪv/` (innovative) and `/ˌtek.nəˈlɒdʒ.ɪ.kəl/` (technological), drawing attention to vowel alternation. Also, "robot" /ɑː/ and "job" /ɑː/. Gestures are used to simulate explaining or assigning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'Let me clarify,' what am I trying to do?" (Expected: Explain something more clearly, make sure everyone understands.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When would it be important to assign roles in a group project about `artificial intelligence`?" (Expected: So everyone knows their `responsibilities`, to avoid `challenges`, to ensure `efficiency`.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students read a short dialogue (B1.2 level) between three students (Ana, Carlos, Maria) who are trying to organize their "AI Debate" project. The dialogue presents a common group conflict where one student is confused about their `role` and another uses `complex` `technological` terms, causing a `misunderstanding`. It includes expressions for clarifying and assigning tasks, and vocabulary related to `AI's impact on society`, `job markets`, and `employment`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Can understand and clarify information, negotiate roles, and resolve basic `misunderstandings` within a group project context about `Intelligent Robots`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify the main `challenge` or `misunderstanding` in the dialogue (gist question).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match each character's initial idea for their `role` with the clarified `role` using visual icons (e.g., a magnifying glass for 'researcher', a microphone for 'presenter').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">True/False: "Ana understood her task from the beginning." "Carlos used simple terms to explain `algorithms`."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on using conditional forms (e.g., "If AI continues to evolve, it might change the job market significantly."), future simple ("AI will create new `employment` `opportunities`."), and passive voice ("AI `systems` are `programmed` to `learn` from `data`.") for clarifying, explaining, and assigning roles in the context of `Intelligent Robots` and `society`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice:</b> Students complete sentences related to group project mediation using provided words. Example: "If we don't ______ (clarify / advance) our `goals`, our project ______ (will fail / might improve)." or "The `software` `updates` ______ (are programmed / will program) to `enhance` `functionality`." They also identify phrases used for clarifying or assigning roles from a list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Practice:</b> Students are given a scenario (e.g., "A classmate doesn't understand the `challenge` `robots` pose to `customer service` `careers`.") and a mediation phrase starter (e.g., "Let me explain that differently...") and they complete the explanation using target vocabulary and grammar. For role assignment, they replace parts of sentences: "How about you [verb: research / design / present] the `societal` `impacts`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check:</b> In pairs, students practice short dialogues where one student presents a slightly confusing idea about `AI's future impact` on `employment`, and the other student uses mediation phrases (e.g., "So, if I understand correctly...", "What I hear you saying is...") to clarify. They also practice suggesting `roles` for their `AI Debate` project using phrases like "Would you prefer to develop arguments for `opportunities` or `challenges`?" Peer feedback focuses on clarity and appropriate language use.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Discussion and Role Assignment Simulation for "AI Debate". Students, in groups of 3-4, will simulate a planning meeting for their upcoming "AI Debate" project on `How Technology Changes Society`. Each group receives a brief prompt with a potential `misunderstanding` or a need for `role` `clarification` (e.g., "One member thinks `AI will create new job markets`, another thinks `jobs will be automated`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group must clearly state their debate `goal` (e.g., "Our project will discuss how `AI` could `drastically` change `jobs` and daily `life`," using a visual timeline for `technological` `evolution`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must resolve the given `misunderstanding` or clarify complex points about `AI's societal impact` using simple terms, gestures, and visual aids (e.g., drawing icons for `employment` sectors). For instance, "AI is a `technology` that is `programmed` to `learn` from `data` and `perform` `tasks` like humans, so `if` it `evolves`, it `might` take over `repetitive jobs`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They will then assign `roles` for the debate (e.g., researcher for `data` on `job markets`, presenter for `societal` `opportunities`, rebuttal specialist for `challenges`), explicitly using mediating language and considering each member's `interests` (e.g., "Would you like to focus on `AI` in education or in the `workplace`?" with icons representing each sector).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the simulation, each group briefly presents their clarified `goal` and `assigned roles` to the class, demonstrating their mediation strategies.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on effective mediation. A peer assessment checklist where students evaluate another group's ability to clarify, explain, and assign roles.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will actively participate in their group's "AI Debate" project planning, demonstrating their ability to mediate `misunderstandings` and negotiate/assign `roles`. During their group presentation to the class, the teacher assesses their mediation skills through observation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Explanations:</b> Resolve `misunderstandings` about `AI's impact` using simple terms and gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Role Assignment:</b> Aid in negotiating `group roles` using visuals or sketches based on `interests`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Goal Clarification:</b> Ensure `group goals` for `AI's societal impacts` are understood with visual aids.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Language Use:</b> Appropriate use of conditional forms, future simple, and passive voice (e.g., "If `AI` continues to `advance`, `job markets` `will shift`.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Consistent use of target `nouns`, `verbs`, and `adjectives` (e.g., `artificial intelligence`, `evolve`, `societal`, `technological`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation, particularly of long vowel /ɑː/ in "robot," "job," and vowel alternation in "innovative," "technological."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active and respectful participation in `mediating` `group discussions`.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one way I helped my group understand a `complex` idea about `AI` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effectively did I help assign `roles` in our `AI Debate` project planning?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one `societal` `challenge` related to `AI` that I feel more confident explaining to someone else now?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I `improve` my ability to `mediate` `discussions` and help assign `roles` in future `collaborative projects`?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of fostering `mediation skills` for the `AI Debate`? The use of visual aids and role-play seemed effective in clarifying `complex` concepts and `assigning roles`. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? Perhaps providing more explicit sentence frames for negotiating `roles` or offering a wider range of vocabulary for describing `AI's` `functionality` for lower-proficiency students. Ensure continued practice of pronunciation, especially the `/ɑː/` sound and vowel alternation, in future lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "How Technology Changes Society" and prepares students for the actual "AI Debate." The next lesson will begin a new theme, perhaps focusing on "Global Challenges" or "Environmental Protection," where students will again apply `communication` and `mediation skills` in different `scenarios`.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>